# Engineer report: Track A Slice L-f (large-module linking)

Created-GMT: 2026-10-08
Model: Claude Opus 5.5
Worktree: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-l-f`, branch `linker-l-f` @ e12765c8
Spec: [`collab/1791405500000-architect-c4-p2-remediation.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`](./1791405500000-architect-c4-p2-remediation.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md) (sections 0, 2, 3, 5)

Status: implemented, lanes run, nothing staged, nothing committed. Awaiting
adversarial review and Architect sign-off.

## 1. What changed

### 1.1 R1, R2: bounds

- [`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc`](../src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc) `default-bounds`
  is `{:max-parts 65536, :max-depth 256, :max-bytes 16777216}`; the docstring
  names the prelude-sized module as the honest image behind the parts figure,
  the byte bound as the finite envelope, and the depth bound as the chain guard.
  `bounded` is unchanged.
- [`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc`](../src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc):
  `publish-module!` gains `[handle spec opts]`, `opts` `{:bounds {...}}`,
  forwarded to both `closure/walk` calls and to every `link-local`. The body
  moved verbatim into a private `publish-closure!` (no re-indentation), and
  `publish-module!` is the two-arity entry. `link-local`'s `:bounds` goes into
  the `local-runtime` options (so `link-state` receives it) and is removed from
  the `link-manifest` opts.
- No test held a literal `4096`, so nothing moved to
  `(:max-parts linker/default-bounds)`.

### 1.2 R3: the derivation tree at steps 2 to 4

Mechanism (section 3.1's first option, plus the verify arity it needs):

- `request-keys` gains `:yin.link/scan?`; `request-link` stores
  `:scan? (not (false? ...))` on the link; `receive-part`, the one place
  `step` calls `verify`, passes it.
- `verify` gains a five-arity `[format identity address parts scan?]`; the
  exported four-arity delegates with `true` and behaves as before. With
  `scan?` false it returns after `validation-defect` with `:value` (and
  `:parts`) and no `:obligations` key.
- `completion` omits `:obligations` when the outcome has none (a step-4
  completion); every scanned completion is unchanged.
- `drive-link` gains a six-arity taking `scan?`; `verified-policy-outcome`
  is its one caller passing `false`.
- `fetch`, `step`, the format records and the step order are untouched; no
  branch on `:format` was added.

### 1.3 R4 and R5: the tree scanners are one walk

R4's free-name walk was implemented first. The section 3.4 measurement then
showed the two other tree scanners far above R5's one-second line
(section 3 below), so R5 was applied to both. The result is one private
walk, `tree-places`, over the occurrence relation:

- worklist `[path id bound in-body? conditional?]` from `[[] root #{} false false]`;
  children from `vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar` exactly as
  `vm/occurrence-child-places` enumerates them (`tree-child-places`, a local
  copy since the vm one is private and `yin.vm` may not change);
- entering a `:lambda`'s slot 3 adds its params to `bound` and sets both
  `in-body?` and `conditional?`; entering an `:if`'s slot 3 or 4 sets
  `conditional?`; nothing else changes the context;
- `loop` with a vector frontier and a transient accumulator, no `for`, no
  multi-key `assoc` (section 3.2's portability rules).

The three scanners filter its places and sort by `path-order`:

- `tree-free-name-occurrences`: a `:variable` whose name is not in `bound`
  and not reserved, `{:name :at [root path] :in-body?}`.
- `tree-definition-occurrences`: `tree-binding` recognises a
  `:vm/store-put` row (own path) and a definition form (`yin/def` operator
  row, two operands, first a `:literal`; path extended by `[3 2]`), carrying
  `conditional?`.
- `tree-application-sites`: an `:application` row at `(conj path [3 n])`.

Deleted from the linker: `tree-occurrence-query`, `tree-definition-query`,
`tree-application-query`, `tree-row-at`, `tree-enclosure`, `tree-rows`, and
the `dao.space.query` require. The walk is occurrence-scoped (per place,
never per row; no visited set), so the shared-row hazard cannot arise.
`vm/free-names`, `vm/occurrence-rules`, `vm/occurrences`,
`vm/ast-requirements` and `dao.space.query` are untouched.

Section 5.5 permitted "whether the walk carries `in-body?` or recomputes it
with `tree-enclosure`"; carrying it was chosen, and carrying `conditional?`
the same way follows from R5's conversion.

### 1.4 R6: the serving composition's policy

[`src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc`](../src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc):
`composition` takes `:derivation` (stored as `(or derivation :verifying)`);
`attempt` forwards it to `link-manifest`; an `:ok` response merges the
outcome's `:derivation` and `:trust` keys (present for the three lowered
formats). `serve`'s and `composition`'s docstrings name the option.
`dht-link` still hard-codes `:verifying` (R6 names only `attempt`).

### 1.5 Tests (step 1)

- [`test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc`](../test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc):
  - fixtures `shared-row` (`((fn [x] x) x)`), `nested-scopes`, and
    `wide-module n` (the wide layout: one application of an n-param lambda
    to n `(yin/def f<i> (fn [] (+ f<i+1> (* f<i+2> f<i+3>))))` forms);
  - `tree-free-name-walk-equals-the-occurrence-rules`: on 20 fixtures (every
    tree fixture of the namespace, the discharge and Rule R ones included,
    `shared-row`, `nested-scopes`) plus `(wide-module 12)`, the walk's record
    set equals the oracle built in the test from `vm/occurrence-rules` with
    the path in `:find`, reserved names removed, `:in-body?` read off the
    occurrence relation independently (`oracle-enclosure`); the shared-row
    case asserts exactly one record, at `[[3 0]]`;
  - `tree-definition-and-application-walks-equal-their-queries` (R5's
    conformance obligation): the same fixtures against the deleted store-key
    and application-row queries, now held in the test as oracles, with
    `:conditional?` from `oracle-enclosure`;
  - `^:slow a-prelude-sized-module-publishes-under-default-bounds`, guarded
    with `dao.test-slow/guard`: `(wide-module 1000)`, 6006 rows, publishes
    complete under the default bounds; the three vector formats link `:ok`
    retaining exactly `#{+ *}`; the tree format: see finding 4.1. No
    wall-clock assertion.
- [`test/yin/vm/linker_manifest_test.cljc`](../test/yin/vm/linker_manifest_test.cljc):
  - `a-verifying-derivation-fetches-the-tree-without-scanning-it`: a
    `counting-ast-format` record (validator and three scanners wrapped) in
    the test's own format map; per vector format the `:verifying` link is
    `:ok`, `:trust :verified`, the tree validator ran, and the three scanner
